                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-30", "sa-data-integration", "A wall screen must show live counts of orders per minute from a stream, updating every few seconds, built without writing an app. What do you recommend?", listOf("An eventstream from Event Hubs into a Fabric eventhouse, shown on a Real-Time Dashboard", "Event Hubs Capture to Data Lake Storage, read by a Power BI report on scheduled refresh", "Azure Monitor metric alerts on the Event Hubs incoming messages, emailed to managers", "A static website in Blob Storage, republished every night by a Data Factory pipeline"), setOf(0), "Fabric Real-Time Intelligence reads the Event Hubs stream with an eventstream and stores it in an eventhouse. A Real-Time Dashboard tile runs a KQL (Kusto Query Language) query (for example, summarize count() by bin(timestamp, 1m)), and live refresh updates the visuals automatically as new data arrives, with no custom app. The older route, a Stream Analytics job with a Power BI output feeding a Power BI streaming dashboard, is deprecated: real-time streaming in Power BI is being retired, and from 31 October 2027 those jobs can't be created and existing ones are stopped. Microsoft recommends Fabric Real-Time Intelligence instead.",
                scenario = "Saltire Pizza's Android ordering app sends each order to Event Hubs. The managers want a live screen in the head office during big football matches.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Capture writes files in batches minutes apart, and scheduled refresh runs at most every 30 minutes, far from every few seconds.",
                    2 to "Alerts send a notice when a threshold is crossed. They do not draw a live screen of counts.",
                    3 to "A static website shows fixed files, and a nightly rebuild is a day late for a live screen.",
                ),
            ),
            // Business continuity: backup and disaster recovery
            B.q("sa-m4-31", "sa-backup-dr", "A faulty batch job overwrote thousands of block blobs in a container this morning. You must roll the container back to how it was at 06:00. What should have been enabled, and used now?", listOf("Point-in-time restore for block blobs, with versioning, change feed and soft delete", "Read-access geo-redundant storage, reading the original files from the secondary", "A CanNotDelete lock on the storage account, with container soft delete", "Hourly share snapshots of the container, restoring the one taken at 06:00"), setOf(0), "Point-in-time restore returns block blobs in chosen containers to their state at an earlier moment. It relies on blob versioning, the change feed and soft delete being turned on.",
                scenario = "Wavebreak Surf's Android app reads surf spot data from Blob Storage. A batch job wrote bad files over the whole container at 07:30.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Geo-replication copies the bad overwrites to the secondary within minutes, so it holds the bad files too.",
                    2 to "The lock stops the account being deleted and container soft delete restores deleted containers. Neither undoes overwritten blobs.",
                    3 to "Share snapshots are an Azure Files feature. Blob containers have no container-level snapshots, only snapshots of single blobs.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-32", "sa-backup-dr", "SQL Server runs on an Azure VM, and the database must be recoverable to within 15 minutes of a failure, with full and log backups managed centrally. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Backup for SQL Server in Azure VMs, with log backups every 15 minutes", "An Enhanced Azure VM backup policy taking snapshots every four hours", "Nightly full backups with BACKUP TO URL into Blob Storage", "Azure Site Recovery replicating the VM to a second region"), setOf(0), "Azure Backup has a workload-aware backup for SQL Server in Azure VMs. It takes full, differential and log backups, with log backups as often as every 15 minutes, and restores to a point in time.",
                scenario = "Haverford Clinics' appointments database, used by its Android booking app, runs on SQL Server in an Azure VM. Losing a day of bookings would be a disaster.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "The Enhanced policy's shortest interval is four hours, so up to four hours of bookings could be lost.",
                    2 to "Nightly backups can lose up to a day of data, and each server's jobs are run separately, not managed centrally.",
                    3 to "Site Recovery replicates the VM for failover. It takes no full and log backups of the database for a point-in-time restore.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-33", "sa-backup-dr", "During failover to the other region, a DNS record must be updated and a load balancer reconfigured automatically, between bringing up the database tier and the web tier. What do you use?", listOf("An Azure Automation runbook added as a step in the Site Recovery recovery plan", "A manual action added between the groups in the recovery plan", "A separate recovery plan group holding the DNS zone and load balancer", "An Azure Policy remediation task that corrects the DNS and load balancer"), setOf(0), "Recovery plans can include scripts or Azure Automation runbooks before or after any group. They run automatically during failover, so steps such as DNS changes happen in the right order without people.",
                scenario = "Glenfield Water's DR test for the back end of its Android outage app took four hours because engineers changed DNS and load balancers by hand in the middle.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "A manual action pauses failover until a person does the work, which is what made the test take four hours.",
                    2 to "Recovery plan groups order the failover of replicated machines. DNS zones and load balancers are not machines, and a group changes nothing on them.",
                    3 to "Policy remediation runs on its own trigger, outside the recovery plan, so it cannot run in order between the database and web tiers.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-34", "sa-backup-dr", "The primary region of a GRS storage account is down, Microsoft has not yet failed over, and the business wants writes back now, accepting some data loss. What do you do?", listOf("Start a customer-managed unplanned failover to the secondary region", "Start a customer-managed planned failover to the secondary region", "Point the app at the read-access secondary endpoint until the primary returns", "Restore yesterday's blob backup into a new account in another region"), setOf(0), "With geo-redundant storage you can start an account failover yourself. The secondary becomes the primary and writes resume. Data not yet copied to the secondary, usually the last few minutes, is lost.",
                scenario = "Bracken Couriers' Android proof-of-delivery app writes photos to a GRS account. The primary region has been down for three hours and drivers cannot complete deliveries.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "A planned failover needs the account available in both regions, which it is not while the primary is down.",
                    2 to "The account is GRS, which has no readable secondary endpoint, and even an RA-GRS secondary accepts no writes.",
                    3 to "A day-old backup loses far more than a failover would.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-35", "sa-backup-dr", "A Cosmos DB account must keep periodic backups, but every two hours instead of four, held for a week. What do you do?", listOf("Change the periodic backup interval and retention on the account", "Switch the account to continuous backup with 7-day retention", "Add a second region to the account as a live copy of the data", "Raise the provisioned throughput so backups can run more often"), setOf(0), "In periodic backup mode you can set how often backups are taken and how long they are kept. A restore from them is requested through Microsoft support.",
                scenario = "Hollowell Games keeps player inventories for its Android game in Cosmos DB in periodic backup mode. Losing four hours of purchases is too much, but the team does not need self-service restore.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Continuous mode replaces periodic backups with self-service point-in-time restore, which the team does not need, and the switch cannot be undone.",
                    2 to "A second region replicates every mistake at once. It is not a backup.",
                    3 to "Backups run in the background without using provisioned throughput, so throughput has nothing to do with how often they run.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-36", "sa-backup-dr", "An Azure SQL database in a failover group must be running in the secondary region within minutes of a regional outage. What should the recovery plan rely on?", listOf("The automatic failover policy, with its default grace period", "A planned failover, which waits until the two databases are in sync", "A manual forced failover started by the team as soon as the outage is confirmed", "A geo-restore of the latest backup"), setOf(2), "A failover group's automatic failover waits for a grace period, an hour by default, before acting, so it alone cannot meet a target of minutes. A forced failover started by the team takes effect quickly, accepting that the last few seconds of data may be lost.",
                scenario = "Galloway Pay's Android wallet must recover within 15 minutes of a regional outage. Its Azure SQL database is in a failover group with a secondary in another region.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Automatic failover waits for its grace period, an hour by default, so the 15-minute target would be missed.",
                    1 to "A planned failover needs the primary to be reachable to synchronise, which it is not during an outage.",
                    3 to "Geo-restore takes much longer and loses more data than failing over to the replica.",
                ),
            ),
            // Business continuity: high availability
            B.q("sa-m4-37", "sa-high-availability", "A single legacy VM cannot be clustered. Which choice gives it the best SLA Microsoft offers for one VM on its own?", listOf("Use Premium SSD or Ultra disks for all its disks", "Use Standard HDD disks", "Put it in an availability set on its own", "Turn on boot diagnostics"), setOf(0), "A single VM gets a 99.9 percent SLA when all its disks are Premium SSD, Premium SSD v2 or Ultra disks. Running two or more VMs across zones or in an availability set gives more, but this app cannot be clustered.",
                scenario = "Westmoor Council runs a licensing server for its Android parking app that cannot run as two copies. The council wants the best uptime promise it can get.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Standard HDD gives a much lower single-VM SLA.",
                    2 to "An availability set protects only when it has two or more VMs.",
                    3 to "Boot diagnostics helps troubleshooting. It has nothing to do with the SLA.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-38", "sa-high-availability", "Users in Asia and America read a database whose primary is in Europe, and reads must be fast for them while the copies can also take over in a disaster. What do you recommend?", listOf("Active geo-replication with readable secondaries in Asia and America", "Read scale-out replicas of the database in West Europe", "A failover group with a single readable secondary in Asia", "Elastic pools in Asia and America sharing resources with the primary"), setOf(0), "Active geo-replication keeps up to four readable secondary databases in other regions. Readers in each region query their nearest copy, and any secondary can be failed over to if the primary region is lost.",
                scenario = "Riverside Language School's Android course app has learners worldwide, with its Azure SQL database in West Europe. Learners far away find lessons slow to load.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Read scale-out replicas sit in the same region as the primary, no nearer to Asia and America.",
                    2 to "One secondary in Asia leaves American learners still reading from Europe.",
                    3 to "Elastic pools share compute among databases on one server. They do not replicate data to other regions.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-39", "sa-high-availability", "The cache behind the app must survive a data centre failure and also serve users in a second region with local reads and writes. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Managed Redis with zone redundancy and active geo-replication", "A Redis server installed on one VM", "An in-memory dictionary in each web server", "Azure Managed Redis with one node and no replication"), setOf(0), "Azure Managed Redis spreads its nodes across availability zones for high availability. Active geo-replication links caches in several regions, each accepting reads and writes, so users get a nearby cache.",
                scenario = "Oriel Games' Android leaderboard reads from a Redis cache. The game is launching in America, and an outage last year emptied the cache for an afternoon.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "One VM is a single point of failure, in one region.",
                    2 to "Separate in-memory copies drift apart and vanish on restart.",
                    3 to "A single node without replication fails with its data centre.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-40", "sa-high-availability", "A Cosmos DB account with one write region and two read regions must lose no committed data if the write region fails. Which consistency level gives that?", listOf("Eventual", "Session", "Strong", "Consistent prefix"), setOf(2), "With Strong consistency, a write is confirmed only when the other regions have it too, so a regional failure loses no committed data. The cost is higher write latency, and it cannot be used with multi-region writes.",
                scenario = "Sterling Exchange's Android trading app records orders in Cosmos DB. The regulator says a confirmed order must never be lost, even in a regional outage.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "Eventual consistency confirms writes before other regions have them, so recent ones can be lost.",
                    1 to "Session consistency guarantees a user sees their own writes, not that other regions have them.",
                    3 to "Consistent prefix keeps order but still confirms before replication.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-41", "sa-high-availability", "SQL Server must run on Azure VMs, and the database must survive the loss of a data centre with automatic failover. What do you recommend?", listOf("An Always On availability group with replicas on VMs in different availability zones", "A single SQL Server VM with zone-redundant Premium SSD disks", "An Always On availability group with both replicas in a proximity placement group", "A failover cluster instance on two VMs in one availability set"), setOf(0), "An Always On availability group keeps copies of the database on several SQL Server VMs and fails over automatically. Placing the VMs in different availability zones means the loss of one data centre leaves a working replica.",
                scenario = "Marchbank's warehouse system needs SQL Server features that tie it to VMs, and its Android picking app stops if the database stops.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Zone-redundant disks survive the loss of a zone, but the one VM does not, and nothing fails over automatically.",
                    2 to "A proximity placement group puts the VMs close together, often in the same data centre, so one failure can take both.",
                    3 to "An availability set spreads VMs across racks within one data centre, so losing the data centre takes both nodes.",
                ),
            ),
            // Infrastructure: compute
            B.q("sa-m4-42", "sa-compute", "One web app is already packaged as a single Linux container, and the team wants managed hosting with custom domains, autoscale and slots, without orchestrating containers. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure App Service for Containers (Web App for Containers)", "Azure Kubernetes Service", "Azure Batch", "Azure Container Instances with no load balancer"), setOf(0), "App Service can run a custom container as a web app, with the platform's custom domains, TLS, autoscale and deployment slots. For one web container there is no orchestrator to learn.",
                scenario = "Bramling's Android gardening app has a single web API container. The two developers have no Kubernetes experience and want staging slots for releases.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "AKS orchestrates many containers and means running a cluster, too much for one container.",
                    2 to "Batch runs compute jobs, not web APIs.",
                    3 to "Container Instances has no slots or built-in autoscale for a web app.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-43", "sa-compute", "Data must stay encrypted even while it is being processed in memory, so that the cloud operator cannot read it. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure confidential VMs", "Standard VMs with disk encryption", "Spot VMs", "VMs in a proximity placement group"), setOf(0), "Confidential VMs run inside hardware-based trusted execution environments that encrypt memory with keys the host cannot read. This protects data in use, not just at rest or in transit.",
                scenario = "Lanyon Genomics processes DNA data for the reports in its Android health app. Its research partners demand that data be protected even while it is being computed.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Disk encryption protects data at rest, not in memory.",
                    2 to "Spot VMs are about price, not protection.",
                    3 to "Placement groups are about latency, not encryption.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-44", "sa-compute", "Twenty Windows Server VMs run around the clock for the next three years, and the company already owns Windows Server licences with Software Assurance. How do you cut their cost the most? Select two.", listOf("Buy reserved instances, or a savings plan, for a three-year term", "Apply the Azure Hybrid Benefit to use the existing Windows Server licences", "Convert them to Spot VMs", "Turn them off at night"), setOf(0, 1), "Reservations and savings plans give large discounts for committing to steady compute for one or three years. The Azure Hybrid Benefit removes the Windows licence cost from the VM price when you bring licences you already own.",
                scenario = "Pryor Shipping's tracking platform behind its Android app runs on 20 VMs that must stay up all the time. Finance wants the lowest cost for the next three years.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    2 to "Spot VMs can be evicted at any time, which a service that must always run cannot accept.",
                    3 to "These VMs must run around the clock.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-45", "sa-compute", "A serverless function must scale to zero and reach a database that has only a private endpoint in a VNet. Which hosting plan do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Functions Flex Consumption plan with virtual network integration", "Azure Functions Consumption plan on Windows with virtual network integration", "Azure Functions Premium plan with virtual network integration", "Azure Logic Apps Consumption with the SQL Server connector"), setOf(0), "The Flex Consumption plan scales to zero, bills per use, and supports virtual network integration, so the function can reach private endpoints. The older Windows Consumption plan has no VNet integration.",
                scenario = "Merlin Garages' Android booking app uses a function to check service slots in a private Azure SQL database. Traffic is light, and cost must stay low when idle.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "The Windows Consumption plan does not support virtual network integration, so it cannot reach the private endpoint.",
                    2 to "The Premium plan supports VNet integration, but it always keeps at least one billed instance, so it never scales to zero.",
                    3 to "Consumption logic apps run workflows, not the team's function code, and multitenant Logic Apps has no virtual network integration to reach the private endpoint.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-46", "sa-compute", "A self-managed database on a VM needs a data disk where IOPS and throughput can be raised and lowered independently of its size, without downtime. Which disk type do you specify?", listOf("Premium SSD v2", "Standard HDD", "Standard SSD", "Ephemeral OS disk"), setOf(0), "Premium SSD v2 lets you set capacity, IOPS and throughput separately and change performance on the fly. You pay for the performance you need instead of buying a bigger disk to get it.",
                scenario = "Calder Analytics runs a self-managed database for its Android dashboard app on an Azure VM. It needs high IOPS at month end but only 100 GB of space.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Standard HDD offers low, fixed performance.",
                    2 to "Standard SSD performance is tied to disk size and is too low for this.",
                    3 to "Ephemeral disks are temporary OS disks and lose data on reimage.",
                ),
            ),
            // Infrastructure: application architecture
            B.q("sa-m4-47", "sa-app-architecture", "The back end must push live score updates to 200,000 connected app users at once over WebSockets, without running its own socket servers. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Web PubSub", "Azure Storage queues", "Azure Batch", "Azure Data Factory"), setOf(0), "Azure Web PubSub is a managed service for real-time messaging over WebSockets. It holds the client connections and lets the back end publish to all of them, or to groups, at scale.",
                scenario = "Kickoff's Android football app shows live scores while the app is open. The team's own WebSocket servers fall over during big matches.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Queues pass work between back-end services. They do not push to open apps.",
                    2 to "Batch runs compute jobs, not live connections.",
                    3 to "Data Factory moves data in pipelines, not to app users in real time.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-48", "sa-app-architecture", "Some messages carry 20 MB video files, larger than the Service Bus Standard message limit, and the team wants to stay on Standard. What design do you recommend?", listOf("The claim-check pattern: each file in Blob Storage, its location in the message", "Send each file as one batch of smaller messages in a single send call", "Compress each file with GZip before putting it in the message body", "The competing consumers pattern, with more receivers on the queue"), setOf(0), "The claim-check pattern puts the large payload in a store such as Blob Storage and sends only a small message pointing at it. The receiver fetches the payload, so the message stays well under the limit.",
                scenario = "Reelhouse's Android video app uploads clips that a back end processes in steps through Service Bus Standard, whose messages are limited to 256 KB.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "On Standard a batch is capped at 256 KB in total, the same as one message, so a 20 MB file still does not fit.",
                    2 to "Video is already compressed, so a 20 MB clip will not shrink to 256 KB.",
                    3 to "More receivers process messages faster but do nothing about the message size limit.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-49", "sa-app-architecture", "API Management must reject calls without a valid Microsoft Entra access token before they reach the back-end API. What do you configure?", listOf("A validate-jwt policy on the API in API Management", "A subscription key sent in a header instead of the query string", "A check-header policy that requires an Authorization header", "A rate-limit-by-key policy keyed on the subscription"), setOf(0), "The validate-jwt policy checks the token on each call: its signature, issuer, audience and expiry. Calls without a valid token are rejected at the gateway and never reach the back end.",
                scenario = "Tarnwell Insurance's Android app calls its claims API through API Management. A penetration test found the API could be called with a subscription key copied from the app.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Moving the key to a header changes nothing: it can still be extracted from the app and identifies no user.",
                    2 to "check-header only tests that a header exists or matches fixed values. It cannot verify a token's signature, issuer or expiry.",
                    3 to "Rate limiting slows abuse but lets unauthenticated calls through.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-50", "sa-app-architecture", "Product images and scripts are fetched millions of times by users worldwide and change rarely. How do you cut load on the origin and speed up delivery?", listOf("Cache them at the edge with Azure Front Door", "Store them in Azure SQL Database", "Add more App Service instances in one region", "Serve them through API Management with no caching"), setOf(0), "Azure Front Door caches static content at Microsoft's edge locations close to users. Most requests are answered from the edge, so they are faster and the origin sees far fewer of them.",
                scenario = "Bluestone Outdoor's website and Android app load product images from App Service in one region. Users in Asia wait seconds for each image.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "A database is the wrong place for images and is no closer to users.",
                    2 to "More instances in one region do not reduce distance to Asia.",
                    3 to "API Management without caching sends every call to the origin.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-51", "sa-app-architecture", "Developers must create their own short-lived test environments from approved templates, within cost and policy limits, without platform team tickets. What do you recommend?", listOf("Approved Bicep template specs that developers deploy by pipeline into a sandbox subscription with Azure Policy and budgets", "Contributor for every developer on a shared non-production subscription, with a monthly cost report to managers", "A ticket to the platform team for each environment, which then runs an approved Bicep pipeline for the requester", "One long-lived shared test environment, deployed from approved Bicep templates and governed by Azure Policy"), setOf(0), "The platform team publishes approved Bicep templates, for example as template specs or built from Azure verified modules. Developers start environments from them through a pipeline into a sandbox subscription. Azure Policy and budgets keep cost and security in check, and a scheduled pipeline (or deleting the deployment stack) removes each environment when it expires. Azure Deployment Environments used to do this job, but it retires on 22 February 2027. Microsoft's retirement guide points to ARM or Bicep, Azure verified modules, and Azure DevOps or GitHub workflows instead.",
                scenario = "Cobalt Health's Android app teams wait a week for each test environment. The platform team wants self-service without losing control of cost and security.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Contributor with no approved templates or policy limits loses control of security, and a monthly report finds overspending only after it happens.",
                    2 to "Tickets are the week-long wait the teams complain about, however automated the pipeline behind them.",
                    3 to "One long-lived shared environment is not self-service or short-lived, and teams trip over each other's changes.",
                ),
            ),
            // Infrastructure: migrations
            B.q("sa-m4-52", "sa-migrations", "The assessment found an old intranet app nobody has used for a year, and a factory control system that must stay on-site for safety reasons. How should each be classed in the migration plan?", listOf("Retire the unused app and retain the factory system on-premises", "Retire the unused app and rehost the factory system to Azure VMs", "Rehost the unused app and retain the factory system on-premises", "Rehost the unused app and the factory system to Azure VMs"), setOf(0), "Rationalising the estate means deciding what to do with each workload. Retire means switching off what is no longer needed. Retain means keeping on-premises what must stay there for now.",
                scenario = "Aldermoor Engineering is planning its move to Azure, starting with the back end of its Android field app. The assessment turned up 30 workloads, including these two.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Retiring the unused app is right, but the factory system must stay on-site for safety.",
                    2 to "Moving an app nobody has used for a year costs money for nothing. It should be retired.",
                    3 to "Moving an unused app costs money for nothing, and the factory system must stay on-site for safety.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-53", "sa-migrations", "VMware VMs must be replicated to Azure without installing agents, checked in a test migration that does not touch production, and then cut over. What do you recommend?", listOf("Agentless replication in Azure Migrate, with a test migration before the final migration", "Azure Site Recovery for VMware, with a test failover before the final failover", "Azure Data Box carrying exported VMDK files, with a test in an isolated network first", "Agent-based replication in Azure Migrate, with a test migration before the final migration"), setOf(0), "Azure Migrate replicates VMware VMs to Azure without agents, using the appliance. A test migration starts copies in an isolated network to check them, and the final migration cuts over when ready.",
                scenario = "Penrose Markets is moving 80 VMware VMs, including the back end of its Android shopping app, and wants to prove each one works in Azure before it switches.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Site Recovery for VMware needs the Mobility service agent installed on every VM, which the requirement rules out.",
                    2 to "Data Box is a one-off offline copy, not replication, so changes made after the export are missing at cutover.",
                    3 to "Agent-based replication needs the Mobility service agent installed on every VM, which the requirement rules out.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-54", "sa-migrations", "Forty terabytes of files must reach Blob Storage within two weeks. The office has a dedicated 1 Gbps link with spare capacity. Which approach fits?", listOf("Copy online with AzCopy over the link", "Order a Data Box, because 40 TB is always too much for the network", "Email the files", "Use Azure Site Recovery"), setOf(0), "A 1 Gbps link moves roughly 10 TB a day at full speed, so 40 TB takes about four or five days with overheads, well within two weeks. An online copy with AzCopy avoids the wait for shipping a device.",
                scenario = "Kittiwake Maps stores map tiles for its Android hiking app on a NAS in its office. The link to Azure is new, dedicated and mostly idle.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Data Box suits slow or busy links. Here the network can do it in days.",
                    2 to "Email cannot carry terabytes.",
                    3 to "Site Recovery replicates VMs for disaster recovery, not file copies.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-55", "sa-migrations", "A SQL Server database must move to Azure SQL Database. A four-hour overnight maintenance window is acceptable. What do you recommend?", listOf("An offline migration with Azure Database Migration Service", "Site Recovery replication of the SQL Server VM", "Copy the MDF file into Blob Storage", "Log Replay Service"), setOf(0), "Database Migration Service copies the schema and data from SQL Server to Azure SQL Database. An offline migration, with the source stopped during the copy, is simple and fits when a downtime window is acceptable.",
                scenario = "Hallgarth Library's catalogue database, used by its Android borrowing app, is 50 GB. The library is closed overnight, so downtime then is fine.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Site Recovery moves the VM, not the database into Azure SQL Database.",
                    2 to "Azure SQL Database cannot attach an MDF file.",
                    3 to "Log Replay Service restores backups onto Managed Instance, not Azure SQL Database.",
                ),
            ),
            // Infrastructure: networks
            B.q("sa-m4-56", "sa-network-connectivity", "The data centre needs 100 Gbps of dedicated connectivity straight into Microsoft's network, with link-layer encryption. What do you recommend?", listOf("ExpressRoute Direct with MACsec", "A site-to-site VPN", "ExpressRoute through a provider at 1 Gbps", "Point-to-site VPN"), setOf(0), "ExpressRoute Direct connects you directly to Microsoft's edge routers at 10 or 100 Gbps. It supports MACsec, which encrypts traffic on the physical link.",
                scenario = "Halberd Research moves petabytes of imaging data that feed its Android lab app between its data centre and Azure. Compliance demands encryption on the link itself.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "VPNs run over the internet and cannot reach 100 Gbps.",
                    2 to "1 Gbps is a hundred times too slow.",
                    3 to "Point-to-site connects single devices, not a data centre.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-m4-57", "sa-network-connectivity", "A partner allows calls only from a fixed list of IP addresses, and the company will keep adding public IPs for new services. How do you give the partner one stable range to allow?", listOf("A public IP prefix, with new public IPs taken from it", "Ask the partner to update its list each time", "Use dynamic public IPs", "Private endpoints"), setOf(0), "A public IP prefix reserves a contiguous block of public addresses. Public IPs created from it all fall in the block, so the partner allows the whole range once.",
                scenario = "Torbay Insurance's services behind its Android app call a partner's pricing API. The partner takes weeks to change its firewall list, and Torbay adds services every month.",
